WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.780
From cost to the right price, and what discounts really cost.

2
00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:08.610
This is the video for Module 2 of Introduction to Pricing and Selling.

3
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:14.090
In Module 1 you found your true cost. Now you turn it into a price.

4
00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:21.480
Cost-plus pricing starts from your cost and adds a chosen percentage. It tells you the lowest price you can accept.

5
00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:26.100
Competitor pricing looks at what similar sellers charge.

6
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:33.540
Value pricing looks at what your product is worth to the customer. Good sellers use all three together.

7
00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:38.690
And before you give any discount, you will know exactly what it costs you.

8
00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:47.640
Say a pack costs you 550 naira and you sell it for 800 naira. Your profit is 250 naira.

9
00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:56.780
Markup compares that profit with your cost. 250 divided by 550 is a markup of about 45%.

10
00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:06.780
Margin compares the same profit with your price. 250 divided by 800 is a margin of about 31%.

11
00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:10.140
Both are correct. Trouble starts when you mix them up.

12
00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:15.670
Here is the method. Start with your true cost per item from Module 1.

13
00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:32.330
To keep a target margin, divide your cost by 1 minus that margin. For a 30% margin on 550 naira, divide 550 by 0.70. That gives about 785 naira.

14
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:38.800
Round to a figure that is easy to pay and give change for, such as 800 naira.

15
00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:45.240
Then recheck. Rounding up is usually safe, but rounding down can eat your margin.

16
00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:57.880
Now the trap. If you simply add 30% to 550 naira, you get 715 naira. Your real margin is only 23%, not 30%.

17
00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:05.960
Next, survey the market. List the sellers your customers could buy from instead of you, and compare like with like.

18
00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:20.260
Ngozi divides each price by the size. Seller A works out at 400 naira per 100 g, Seller B at 360 naira, and Seller C, with a branded tub, at 600 naira.

19
00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:34.330
Her own 200 g pack at 800 naira is 400 naira per 100 g. She sits in the lower middle of the range. She is not too expensive, and better packaging could move her up.

20
00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:42.640
Value is what the customer gains, not what you put in. Convenience is one source, like delivery to the office.

21
00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.990
Quality is another: fresher food, neater stitching.

22
00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.100
Reliability matters too. You always deliver what you promise.

23
00:02:53.350 --> 00:03:06.150
So do personal service and trust. For example, Amaka the tailor delivers in 3 days with a proper fitting, so she charges 18,000 naira for a gown that costs her 11,000 naira.

24
00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:10.130
Charge only for real benefits. Never exaggerate.

25
00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:15.070
A discount comes straight out of your profit, not out of your cost.

26
00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:32.590
If Ngozi gives 10% off, her price falls by 80 naira to 720 naira. Her cost stays the same, so her profit falls from 250 naira to 170 naira. She has lost 32% of her profit on that pack.

27
00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:43.510
Here is the extra sales rule. Divide the old profit by the new profit. 250 divided by 170 is 1.47.

28
00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:50.810
So if she normally sells 100 packs a week, she must sell about 147 packs just to stand still.

29
00:03:52.060 --> 00:04:04.880
Give a discount only when it is paid for by a real saving, like one delivery for a bulk order. A free item costs you its cost, not its price, so buy 10 get 1 free can beat 10% off.

30
00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:09.560
Set time limits, write your walk-away price, and never go below your floor.

31
00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:18.540
To remember. Price for a margin by dividing cost by 1 minus the margin. Markup and margin are not the same.

32
00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:28.740
Cost sets the floor, competitors show the range, and value places you in it. Discounts come out of profit, so check the extra sales you would need first.

33
00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:37.030
Now work out your own cost-plus price, check five competitors, and test a discount on paper before you offer one.

34
00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:39.320
Continue with the lessons below.
